  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 342 AM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nDry conditions with seasonably warm days will persist into\nFriday as surface ridging holds across much of the eastern CONUS.\nIncreasing drought conditions and RHs dipping into the 20-30 \npercent range will result in elevated concerns for grass fire \ntype activity into Thursday, but continued generally light wind \nwill mitigate a more serious situation with unpredictable fire \nspread. Daytime RHs will trend upward Friday into Saturday,\nbeginning to limit meteorological contributions to fire danger.\n\nHeading into the weekend, rain chances will begin to increase\nacross the area in advance of an upper shortwave shifting into the\nMid MS Valley. Scattered showers will be possible as early as \nFriday night, with any heavier rain or thunderstorms likely to \nhold off until Saturday into Sunday. At this point, run to run \nconsistency in recent forecast guidance with the strength of the \nupper wave, and consequently rainfall amounts/coverage, is poor. \nWhile chances for measurable rain have increased in the official \nforecast, and it is possible a few inches of beneficial rain will \nfall in some portion of our area this weekend, the higher amounts \nmay be more localized in nature from convection. On the opposite \nside of the coin, some areas may see very little rain through this\ntime frame, with drought busting rain not at all a guarantee at\nthis stage. Still, anyone with outdoor plans over the holiday \nweekend should monitor later forecasts closely and have \ncontingencies in mind for rain and possible storms.\n\nFollowing the weekend system, shortwave ridging will quickly shift\nacross the region, followed by what currently looks to be a more\norganized upper trough bringing another storm system into the area\naround the middle of next week, arriving in the Tuesday/Wednesday\ntime frame. If this system pans out as currently depicted, \nstronger deep shear may provide some potential for strong storms. \nHowever, at this forecast range and given recent model \ninconsistencies, it is too soon to much confidence in any further\ndetails. /DL/",
